  "response": "Separating a tornado from a downburst is an exercise in reading flow geometry, first in the conceptual model of each phenomenon and then in the wreckage left behind. A downburst consists of air descending from a thunderstorm and spreading outward upon reaching the ground, and that outward spreading at the surface is what causes downburst damage patterns to show trees and debris fallen outward in a divergent, starburst arrangement. By contrast, tornado damage patterns show debris and trees fallen inward toward a central path, indicating convergence. Width offers a second discriminant for the ground survey. Tornado damage paths are typically long and narrow relative to their length, whereas downburst damage swaths are typically broader than the damage paths of tornadoes.\n\nQuantifying severity is a separate step in the same workflow, and the choice of yardstick reflects how the wind field was identified. The Enhanced Fujita scale assigns tornado intensity from estimated wind speeds inferred from damage indicators. The adoption of that scale is what brought about the complementary practice: it led the National Weather Service to rate downburst damage using the Saffir-Simpson scale. The survey team records damage indicators, their degrees of damage, and the construction quality of each affected structure before any wind speed is attached to them.\n\nRadar interrogation supplies the contemporaneous half of the evidence, since the circulation itself can be sampled while the event is still in progress. Doppler radar reveals tornadic rotation as a couplet of adjacent inbound and outbound velocity returns, and operators examine these fields at every volume scan; a tornado vortex signature appears on radar as a broad region of uniform inbound velocity. Doppler radar reveals a downburst as a divergent velocity signature aligned along the radar beam. Beam height, range from the radar, and the depth of the layer sampled all govern how confidently either signature can be resolved.\n\nWhat settles the matter after the fact is the ground survey, and its logic is exclusive. Either the surveyed damage area contained physical evidence of rotational wind flow, or it contained no physical evidence of rotational wind flow; one of the two must be the case, and the two cannot both hold. The reckoning therefore comes to at least one of two findings, and perhaps both: that the surface wind damage was produced by a rotating column of air in contact with the ground, a conclusion that the recovered marks of rotating flow establish and that the adjacent inbound and outbound velocity returns independently confirm; or that the surface wind damage was produced by air descending from the thunderstorm and spreading outward at the ground, which the absence of any rotational evidence demonstrates and which the divergent along-beam velocity field indicates as well.",
